{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.c6863c2987","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-357","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Fenwick is in western Scotland. It was first surveyed in 1997.</li><li>Its surface area is 696 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Brookhaven is in western Kenya. It was first surveyed in 1904.</li><li>Its surface area is 162 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are freshwater lakes formed by glaciers.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between Lake Fenwick and Lake Brookhaven.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The surface area of Lake Fenwick (696 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Brookhaven (162 square kilometers).","Both Lake Fenwick and Lake Brookhaven are freshwater lakes formed by glaciers.","Lake Fenwick is in western Scotland, and Lake Brookhaven is in western Kenya.","Lake Fenwick was first surveyed in 1997, while Lake Brookhaven was first surveyed in 1904."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.1e74ebd9db","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-359","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Sable Observatory is in southern New Zealand. It began operating in 1915.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 8 meters.</li><li>The Umber Observatory is in western Scotland. It began operating in 1899.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 6 meters.</li><li>Both are radio observatories.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the main telescope's mirror diameter of the two observatorys differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Sable Observatory began operating in 1915, while the Umber Observatory began operating in 1899.","The Sable Observatory, which is in southern New Zealand, is one of several radio observatories.","The main telescope's mirror diameter of the Sable Observatory (8 meters) is greater than that of the Umber Observatory (6 meters).","Both the Sable Observatory and the Umber Observatory are radio observatories."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the main telescope's mirror diameter of both observatorys. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one observatory."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.a529afe8f3","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-361","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Merrow Tunnel is in southern Norway. It opened in 1902.</li><li>Its length is 55 kilometers.</li><li>The Sable Tunnel is in southern Brazil. It opened in 2006.</li><li>Its length is 43 kilometers.</li><li>Both are tunnels that pass under a mountain range.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the Merrow Tunnel and the Sable Tunnel.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Merrow Tunnel opened in 1902, while the Sable Tunnel opened in 2006.","The Merrow Tunnel is in southern Norway, and the Sable Tunnel is in southern Brazil.","Both the Merrow Tunnel and the Sable Tunnel are tunnels that pass under a mountain range.","The length of the Merrow Tunnel (55 kilometers) is greater than that of the Sable Tunnel (43 kilometers)."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.f7ae495937","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-363","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Mount Halden is in eastern Australia. It was first climbed in 2011.</li><li>Its height is 3,565 meters.</li><li>Mount Merrow is in northern Japan. It was first climbed in 1974.</li><li>Its height is 2,013 meters.</li><li>Both are dormant volcanoes.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between Mount Halden and Mount Merrow.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Mount Halden is in eastern Australia, and Mount Merrow is in northern Japan.","Both Mount Halden and Mount Merrow are dormant volcanoes.","The height of Mount Halden (3,565 meters) is greater than that of Mount Merrow (2,013 meters).","Mount Halden was first climbed in 2011, while Mount Merrow was first climbed in 1974."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.13b94186e4","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-364","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Mount Pembrook is in eastern Australia. It was first climbed in 1896.</li><li>Its height is 4,855 meters.</li><li>Mount Glenrock is in eastern Poland. It was first climbed in 2017.</li><li>Its height is 2,124 meters.</li><li>Both are dormant volcanoes.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between Mount Pembrook and Mount Glenrock.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both Mount Pembrook and Mount Glenrock are dormant volcanoes.","Mount Pembrook was first climbed in 1896, while Mount Glenrock was first climbed in 2017.","The height of Mount Pembrook (4,855 meters) is greater than that of Mount Glenrock (2,124 meters).","Mount Pembrook is in eastern Australia, and Mount Glenrock is in eastern Poland."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.4e2434edb7","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-365","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Sable Tower is in western Kenya. It was completed in 2019.</li><li>Its height is 431 meters.</li><li>The Pembrook Tower is in central Spain. It was completed in 2003.</li><li>Its height is 382 meters.</li><li>Both are observation towers open to the public.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the height of the two towers differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The height of the Sable Tower (431 meters) is greater than that of the Pembrook Tower (382 meters).","Both the Sable Tower and the Pembrook Tower are observation towers open to the public.","The Sable Tower was completed in 2019, while the Pembrook Tower was completed in 2003.","The Sable Tower, which is in western Kenya, is one of several observation towers open to the public."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the height of both towers. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one tower."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.85fc2b147e","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-368","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Quarry Tunnel is in eastern Australia. It opened in 1893.</li><li>Its length is 17 kilometers.</li><li>The Cresthill Tunnel is in northern Chile. It opened in 1917.</li><li>Its length is 5 kilometers.</li><li>Both are railway tunnels.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the Quarry Tunnel and the Cresthill Tunnel.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both the Quarry Tunnel and the Cresthill Tunnel are railway tunnels.","The Quarry Tunnel is in eastern Australia, and the Cresthill Tunnel is in northern Chile.","The Quarry Tunnel opened in 1893, while the Cresthill Tunnel opened in 1917.","The length of the Quarry Tunnel (17 kilometers) is greater than that of the Cresthill Tunnel (5 kilometers)."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.9476647060","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-368","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Quarry Tunnel is in eastern Australia. It opened in 1893.</li><li>Its length is 17 kilometers.</li><li>The Cresthill Tunnel is in northern Chile. It opened in 1917.</li><li>Its length is 5 kilometers.</li><li>Both are railway tunnels.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the length of the two tunnels differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both the Quarry Tunnel and the Cresthill Tunnel are railway tunnels.","The Quarry Tunnel opened in 1893, while the Cresthill Tunnel opened in 1917.","The Quarry Tunnel, which is in eastern Australia, is one of several railway tunnels.","The length of the Quarry Tunnel (17 kilometers) is greater than that of the Cresthill Tunnel (5 kilometers)."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the length of both tunnels. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one tunnel."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.81e83c1b8d","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-370","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Mount Fenwick is in western Scotland. It was first climbed in 2011.</li><li>Its height is 3,274 meters.</li><li>Mount Sable is in southern Brazil. It was first climbed in 1999.</li><li>Its height is 2,694 meters.</li><li>Both are dormant volcanoes.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between Mount Fenwick and Mount Sable.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Mount Fenwick is in western Scotland, and Mount Sable is in southern Brazil.","Both Mount Fenwick and Mount Sable are dormant volcanoes.","Mount Fenwick was first climbed in 2011, while Mount Sable was first climbed in 1999.","The height of Mount Fenwick (3,274 meters) is greater than that of Mount Sable (2,694 meters)."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.ab5e5f2b6e","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-371","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Orla National Park is in central Canada. It was established in 1990.</li><li>Its area is 1,115 square kilometers.</li><li>Umber National Park is in western Kenya. It was established in 1930.</li><li>Its area is 206 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are parks that protect coastal wetlands.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the area of the two parks differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both Orla National Park and Umber National Park are parks that protect coastal wetlands.","Orla National Park was established in 1990, while Umber National Park was established in 1930.","Orla National Park, which is in central Canada, is one of several parks that protect coastal wetlands.","The area of Orla National Park (1,115 square kilometers) is greater than that of Umber National Park (206 square kilometers)."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the area of both parks. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one park."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.54bc7b8f3e","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-375","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Thornbury is in central Canada. It was first surveyed in 1936.</li><li>Its surface area is 636 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Wexford is in northern India. It was first surveyed in 1985.</li><li>Its surface area is 500 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are freshwater lakes formed by glaciers.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two lakes were first surveyed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Lake Thornbury was first surveyed in 1936, while Lake Wexford was first surveyed in 1985.","The surface area of Lake Thornbury (636 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Wexford (500 square kilometers).","Both Lake Thornbury and Lake Wexford are freshwater lakes formed by glaciers.","Lake Thornbury is in central Canada, and Lake Wexford is in northern India."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.b1aa4160b6","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-377","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Glenrock Observatory is in northern Chile. It began operating in 1909.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 11 meters.</li><li>The Eastwick Observatory is in southern New Zealand. It began operating in 1963.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 2 meters.</li><li>Both are observatories located on remote mountaintops.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the Glenrock Observatory and the Eastwick Observatory.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The main telescope's mirror diameter of the Glenrock Observatory (11 meters) is greater than that of the Eastwick Observatory (2 meters).","Both the Glenrock Observatory and the Eastwick Observatory are observatories located on remote mountaintops.","The Glenrock Observatory is in northern Chile, and the Eastwick Observatory is in southern New Zealand.","The Glenrock Observatory began operating in 1909, while the Eastwick Observatory began operating in 1963."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.8fab8cf966","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-380","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Orla Observatory is in northern India. It began operating in 1966.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 10 meters.</li><li>The Cresthill Observatory is in eastern Australia. It began operating in 2010.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 7 meters.</li><li>Both are radio observatories.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the Orla Observatory and the Cresthill Observatory.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Orla Observatory began operating in 1966, while the Cresthill Observatory began operating in 2010.","Both the Orla Observatory and the Cresthill Observatory are radio observatories.","The Orla Observatory is in northern India, and the Cresthill Observatory is in eastern Australia.","The main telescope's mirror diameter of the Orla Observatory (10 meters) is greater than that of the Cresthill Observatory (7 meters)."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.b1bf5ef159","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-381","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Merrow National Park is in central Canada. It was established in 1970.</li><li>Its area is 6,494 square kilometers.</li><li>Brookhaven National Park is in western Scotland. It was established in 1901.</li><li>Its area is 4,226 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are parks that protect coastal wetlands.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the area of the two parks differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The area of Merrow National Park (6,494 square kilometers) is greater than that of Brookhaven National Park (4,226 square kilometers).","Merrow National Park was established in 1970, while Brookhaven National Park was established in 1901.","Merrow National Park, which is in central Canada, is one of several parks that protect coastal wetlands.","Both Merrow National Park and Brookhaven National Park are parks that protect coastal wetlands."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the area of both parks. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one park."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.c9107b8af4","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-383","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Mount Eastwick is in northern Chile. It was first climbed in 1927.</li><li>Its height is 6,300 meters.</li><li>Mount Juniper is in northern Japan. It was first climbed in 2015.</li><li>Its height is 3,816 meters.</li><li>Both are peaks in the same mountain range.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two mountains were first climbed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both Mount Eastwick and Mount Juniper are peaks in the same mountain range.","Mount Eastwick is in northern Chile, and Mount Juniper is in northern Japan.","Mount Eastwick was first climbed in 1927, while Mount Juniper was first climbed in 2015.","The height of Mount Eastwick (6,300 meters) is greater than that of Mount Juniper (3,816 meters)."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.efa9b67102","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-389","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Redfern Bridge is in southern New Zealand. It opened in 1984.</li><li>Its main span is 1,583 meters.</li><li>The Northam Bridge is in eastern Poland. It opened in 1921.</li><li>Its main span is 1,528 meters.</li><li>Both are suspension bridges.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the main span of the two bridges differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Redfern Bridge, which is in southern New Zealand, is one of several suspension bridges.","The Redfern Bridge opened in 1984, while the Northam Bridge opened in 1921.","Both the Redfern Bridge and the Northam Bridge are suspension bridges.","The main span of the Redfern Bridge (1,583 meters) is greater than that of the Northam Bridge (1,528 meters)."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the main span of both bridges. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one bridge."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.7a06d16079","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-391","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Quarry Observatory is in northern Chile. It began operating in 2015.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 8 meters.</li><li>The Sable Observatory is in western Kenya. It began operating in 1955.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 6 meters.</li><li>Both are radio observatories.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two observatorys began operating.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both the Quarry Observatory and the Sable Observatory are radio observatories.","The Quarry Observatory is in northern Chile, and the Sable Observatory is in western Kenya.","The main telescope's mirror diameter of the Quarry Observatory (8 meters) is greater than that of the Sable Observatory (6 meters).","The Quarry Observatory began operating in 2015, while the Sable Observatory began operating in 1955."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.4f8d742554","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-394","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Halden is in eastern Poland. It was first surveyed in 1911.</li><li>Its surface area is 854 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Dunmore is in central Spain. It was first surveyed in 1978.</li><li>Its surface area is 658 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are reservoirs created by dams.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two lakes were first surveyed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both Lake Halden and Lake Dunmore are reservoirs created by dams.","The surface area of Lake Halden (854 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Dunmore (658 square kilometers).","Lake Halden is in eastern Poland, and Lake Dunmore is in central Spain.","Lake Halden was first surveyed in 1911, while Lake Dunmore was first surveyed in 1978."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.b9d2b2dcf5","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-396","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Sable is in southern Norway. It was first surveyed in 1904.</li><li>Its surface area is 663 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Valewood is in northern India. It was first surveyed in 2007.</li><li>Its surface area is 313 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are reservoirs created by dams.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two lakes were first surveyed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Lake Sable was first surveyed in 1904, while Lake Valewood was first surveyed in 2007.","Lake Sable is in southern Norway, and Lake Valewood is in northern India.","Both Lake Sable and Lake Valewood are reservoirs created by dams.","The surface area of Lake Sable (663 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Valewood (313 square kilometers)."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.52b671830a","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-2","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Jonas Rahman is a marine biologist.</li><li>Rahman wanted to find out whether noise from passing ships affects how often humpback whales sing.</li><li>Rahman recorded whale songs in a busy shipping lane and in a quiet bay for two months.</li><li>Rahman found that whales in the shipping lane sang about 40 percent less often.</li><li>Rahman concluded that ship noise may interfere with whale communication.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Rahman found that whales in the shipping lane sang about 40 percent less often.","To investigate the question, Rahman recorded whale songs in a busy shipping lane and in a quiet bay for two months.","Marine biologist Jonas Rahman set out to learn whether noise from passing ships affects how often humpback whales sing.","Rahman concluded that ship noise may interfere with whale communication."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.7f2c7d2fcb","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-9","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Sofia Rahman is an ecologist.</li><li>Rahman wanted to find out whether city lights change when songbirds begin singing in the morning.</li><li>Rahman compared the first songs of robins in brightly lit city parks with those in dark rural woodlands.</li><li>Rahman found that robins in lit parks began singing nearly an hour earlier.</li><li>Rahman concluded that artificial light may shift birds' daily rhythms.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Ecologist Sofia Rahman set out to learn whether city lights change when songbirds begin singing in the morning.","To investigate the question, Rahman compared the first songs of robins in brightly lit city parks with those in dark rural woodlands.","Rahman concluded that artificial light may shift birds' daily rhythms.","Rahman found that robins in lit parks began singing nearly an hour earlier."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.72315b9536","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-12","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Amara Asante is a psychologist.</li><li>Asante wanted to find out whether taking short breaks improves students' focus during long study sessions.</li><li>Asante had one group of students take a five-minute break every half hour while another group studied without breaks.</li><li>Asante found that students who took breaks answered more practice questions correctly.</li><li>Asante concluded that brief breaks may help people stay focused.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Asante found that students who took breaks answered more practice questions correctly.","To investigate the question, Asante had one group of students take a five-minute break every half hour while another group studied without breaks.","Asante concluded that brief breaks may help people stay focused.","Psychologist Amara Asante set out to learn whether taking short breaks improves students' focus during long study sessions."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.9aa541c7b8","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-18","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Diego Haddad is an agricultural scientist.</li><li>Haddad wanted to find out whether planting clover between rows of corn reduces the need for fertilizer.</li><li>Haddad grew corn with and without clover planted between the rows for three seasons.</li><li>Haddad found that fields with clover produced similar yields using a third less fertilizer.</li><li>Haddad concluded that clover may supply some of the nutrients corn needs.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Agricultural scientist Diego Haddad set out to learn whether planting clover between rows of corn reduces the need for fertilizer.","Haddad concluded that clover may supply some of the nutrients corn needs.","To investigate the question, Haddad grew corn with and without clover planted between the rows for three seasons.","Haddad found that fields with clover produced similar yields using a third less fertilizer."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.18f7b790e2","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-24","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ingrid Nakamura is a materials scientist.</li><li>Nakamura wanted to find out whether a coating made from seaweed can keep fruit fresh longer.</li><li>Nakamura coated half of a batch of strawberries with a seaweed-based film and left the rest uncoated.</li><li>Nakamura found that coated strawberries stayed fresh for about four days longer.</li><li>Nakamura concluded that the coating could help reduce food waste.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Nakamura found that coated strawberries stayed fresh for about four days longer.","Materials scientist Ingrid Nakamura set out to learn whether a coating made from seaweed can keep fruit fresh longer.","Nakamura concluded that the coating could help reduce food waste.","To investigate the question, Nakamura coated half of a batch of strawberries with a seaweed-based film and left the rest uncoated."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.2eb2819b94","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-28","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ayumi Okafor is a materials scientist.</li><li>Okafor wanted to find out whether a coating made from seaweed can keep fruit fresh longer.</li><li>Okafor coated half of a batch of strawberries with a seaweed-based film and left the rest uncoated.</li><li>Okafor found that coated strawberries stayed fresh for about four days longer.</li><li>Okafor concluded that the coating could help reduce food waste.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Okafor concluded that the coating could help reduce food waste.","Materials scientist Ayumi Okafor set out to learn whether a coating made from seaweed can keep fruit fresh longer.","Okafor found that coated strawberries stayed fresh for about four days longer.","To investigate the question, Okafor coated half of a batch of strawberries with a seaweed-based film and left the rest uncoated."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.9ba71c9e11","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-32","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Omar Asante is an entomologist.</li><li>Asante wanted to find out whether bees prefer flowers of a particular color.</li><li>Asante set out identical feeders colored blue, yellow, and white in a meadow.</li><li>Asante found that bees visited the blue feeders most often.</li><li>Asante concluded that color may play a role in how bees choose flowers.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Asante found that bees visited the blue feeders most often.","Entomologist Omar Asante set out to learn whether bees prefer flowers of a particular color.","To investigate the question, Asante set out identical feeders colored blue, yellow, and white in a meadow.","Asante concluded that color may play a role in how bees choose flowers."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.718326c432","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-33","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Idris Solberg is an entomologist.</li><li>Solberg wanted to find out whether bees prefer flowers of a particular color.</li><li>Solberg set out identical feeders colored blue, yellow, and white in a meadow.</li><li>Solberg found that bees visited the blue feeders most often.</li><li>Solberg concluded that color may play a role in how bees choose flowers.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Solberg set out identical feeders colored blue, yellow, and white in a meadow.","Solberg concluded that color may play a role in how bees choose flowers.","Solberg found that bees visited the blue feeders most often.","Entomologist Idris Solberg set out to learn whether bees prefer flowers of a particular color."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.1abba4ddab","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-44","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ravi Takeda is a geologist.</li><li>Takeda wanted to find out whether a hillside's plant cover affects how much soil washes away in heavy rain.</li><li>Takeda measured soil runoff on bare slopes and on slopes covered with native grasses.</li><li>Takeda found that bare slopes lost several times more soil.</li><li>Takeda concluded that plant cover may protect hillsides from erosion.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Takeda found that bare slopes lost several times more soil.","To investigate the question, Takeda measured soil runoff on bare slopes and on slopes covered with native grasses.","Takeda concluded that plant cover may protect hillsides from erosion.","Geologist Ravi Takeda set out to learn whether a hillside's plant cover affects how much soil washes away in heavy rain."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.d322021574","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-48","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Dmitri Varga is a nutrition researcher.</li><li>Varga wanted to find out whether eating breakfast affects how much food people eat at lunch.</li><li>Varga asked volunteers to eat breakfast on some days and skip it on others, then measured their lunch portions.</li><li>Varga found that lunch portions were about the same on both kinds of days.</li><li>Varga concluded that skipping breakfast may not lead people to eat more later.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Varga found that lunch portions were about the same on both kinds of days.","To investigate the question, Varga asked volunteers to eat breakfast on some days and skip it on others, then measured their lunch portions.","Varga concluded that skipping breakfast may not lead people to eat more later.","Nutrition researcher Dmitri Varga set out to learn whether eating breakfast affects how much food people eat at lunch."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.638ad3b4a8","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-90","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Amara Varga is a hydrologist.</li><li>Varga wanted to find out whether wetlands reduce flooding downstream.</li><li>Varga compared river levels below restored wetlands with levels below drained farmland after storms.</li><li>Varga found that river levels rose more slowly below the wetlands.</li><li>Varga concluded that wetlands may help protect nearby communities from floods.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Varga found that river levels rose more slowly below the wetlands.","Varga concluded that wetlands may help protect nearby communities from floods.","To investigate the question, Varga compared river levels below restored wetlands with levels below drained farmland after storms.","Hydrologist Amara Varga set out to learn whether wetlands reduce flooding downstream."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.06d3039901","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-105","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Amara Kowalski is a chemist.</li><li>Kowalski wanted to find out whether a new plant-based plastic breaks down in seawater.</li><li>Kowalski placed samples of the plastic in tanks of seawater for six months.</li><li>Kowalski found that most samples broke apart within four months.</li><li>Kowalski concluded that the plastic may be less harmful to oceans than conventional plastic.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Chemist Amara Kowalski set out to learn whether a new plant-based plastic breaks down in seawater.","Kowalski concluded that the plastic may be less harmful to oceans than conventional plastic.","To investigate the question, Kowalski placed samples of the plastic in tanks of seawater for six months.","Kowalski found that most samples broke apart within four months."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.fddd8b69a6","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-114","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Beatriz Mensah is a sleep researcher.</li><li>Mensah wanted to find out whether using screens before bed affects how quickly people fall asleep.</li><li>Mensah asked volunteers to read either a printed book or a tablet for an hour before bed.</li><li>Mensah found that tablet readers took about ten minutes longer to fall asleep.</li><li>Mensah concluded that screen use before bed may delay sleep.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Mensah asked volunteers to read either a printed book or a tablet for an hour before bed.","Mensah found that tablet readers took about ten minutes longer to fall asleep.","Mensah concluded that screen use before bed may delay sleep.","Sleep researcher Beatriz Mensah set out to learn whether using screens before bed affects how quickly people fall asleep."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.a9225f87fa","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-117","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Elias Rahman is a sleep researcher.</li><li>Rahman wanted to find out whether using screens before bed affects how quickly people fall asleep.</li><li>Rahman asked volunteers to read either a printed book or a tablet for an hour before bed.</li><li>Rahman found that tablet readers took about ten minutes longer to fall asleep.</li><li>Rahman concluded that screen use before bed may delay sleep.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Rahman found that tablet readers took about ten minutes longer to fall asleep.","Sleep researcher Elias Rahman set out to learn whether using screens before bed affects how quickly people fall asleep.","To investigate the question, Rahman asked volunteers to read either a printed book or a tablet for an hour before bed.","Rahman concluded that screen use before bed may delay sleep."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.b44092fdda","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-119","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ingrid Delacroix is a sleep researcher.</li><li>Delacroix wanted to find out whether using screens before bed affects how quickly people fall asleep.</li><li>Delacroix asked volunteers to read either a printed book or a tablet for an hour before bed.</li><li>Delacroix found that tablet readers took about ten minutes longer to fall asleep.</li><li>Delacroix concluded that screen use before bed may delay sleep.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Delacroix concluded that screen use before bed may delay sleep.","To investigate the question, Delacroix asked volunteers to read either a printed book or a tablet for an hour before bed.","Delacroix found that tablet readers took about ten minutes longer to fall asleep.","Sleep researcher Ingrid Delacroix set out to learn whether using screens before bed affects how quickly people fall asleep."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.1fc99183eb","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-128","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Zara Moreau is a public health researcher.</li><li>Moreau wanted to find out whether adding bike lanes increases how often residents cycle.</li><li>Moreau counted cyclists on streets before and after new bike lanes were added.</li><li>Moreau found that cycling on those streets nearly doubled within a year.</li><li>Moreau concluded that bike lanes may encourage more people to ride.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Moreau concluded that bike lanes may encourage more people to ride.","Moreau found that cycling on those streets nearly doubled within a year.","Public health researcher Zara Moreau set out to learn whether adding bike lanes increases how often residents cycle.","To investigate the question, Moreau counted cyclists on streets before and after new bike lanes were added."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.eba5ed4222","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-134","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ravi Duarte is an archaeologist.</li><li>Duarte wanted to find out whether a coastal settlement traded with inland villages.</li><li>Duarte analyzed the minerals in pottery found at the settlement.</li><li>Duarte found that many pots were made from clay found only far inland.</li><li>Duarte concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Duarte concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities.","To investigate the question, Duarte analyzed the minerals in pottery found at the settlement.","Archaeologist Ravi Duarte set out to learn whether a coastal settlement traded with inland villages.","Duarte found that many pots were made from clay found only far inland."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.3402568f52","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-137","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Mateo Quintero is an archaeologist.</li><li>Quintero wanted to find out whether a coastal settlement traded with inland villages.</li><li>Quintero analyzed the minerals in pottery found at the settlement.</li><li>Quintero found that many pots were made from clay found only far inland.</li><li>Quintero concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Quintero analyzed the minerals in pottery found at the settlement.","Quintero found that many pots were made from clay found only far inland.","Archaeologist Mateo Quintero set out to learn whether a coastal settlement traded with inland villages.","Quintero concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.222d6873be","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-146","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Hana Iyer is a marine ecologist.</li><li>Iyer wanted to find out whether sea urchins limit the growth of kelp forests.</li><li>Iyer fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.</li><li>Iyer found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser.</li><li>Iyer concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Iyer concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.","To investigate the question, Iyer fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.","Marine ecologist Hana Iyer set out to learn whether sea urchins limit the growth of kelp forests.","Iyer found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.40414f1ee7","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-150","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Chen Varga is a climate scientist.</li><li>Varga wanted to find out whether light-colored roofs reduce indoor temperatures.</li><li>Varga compared indoor temperatures in homes with white roofs and homes with dark roofs.</li><li>Varga found that homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler.</li><li>Varga concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Varga found that homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler.","To investigate the question, Varga compared indoor temperatures in homes with white roofs and homes with dark roofs.","Varga concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning.","Climate scientist Chen Varga set out to learn whether light-colored roofs reduce indoor temperatures."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.9c270f583b","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-152","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kofi Abara is a climate scientist.</li><li>Abara wanted to find out whether light-colored roofs reduce indoor temperatures.</li><li>Abara compared indoor temperatures in homes with white roofs and homes with dark roofs.</li><li>Abara found that homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler.</li><li>Abara concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Abara compared indoor temperatures in homes with white roofs and homes with dark roofs.","Abara found that homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler.","Climate scientist Kofi Abara set out to learn whether light-colored roofs reduce indoor temperatures.","Abara concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.08461fbd4e","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-156","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Elias Takeda is a behavioral economist.</li><li>Takeda wanted to find out whether shoppers buy more fruit when it is placed near the store entrance.</li><li>Takeda moved the fruit display to the entrance in some stores but not others.</li><li>Takeda found that fruit sales rose in the stores with entrance displays.</li><li>Takeda concluded that product placement may influence shoppers' choices.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Takeda moved the fruit display to the entrance in some stores but not others.","Takeda found that fruit sales rose in the stores with entrance displays.","Behavioral economist Elias Takeda set out to learn whether shoppers buy more fruit when it is placed near the store entrance.","Takeda concluded that product placement may influence shoppers' choices."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.e2471cb7c8","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-156","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Elias Takeda is a behavioral economist.</li><li>Takeda wanted to find out whether shoppers buy more fruit when it is placed near the store entrance.</li><li>Takeda moved the fruit display to the entrance in some stores but not others.</li><li>Takeda found that fruit sales rose in the stores with entrance displays.</li><li>Takeda concluded that product placement may influence shoppers' choices.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Behavioral economist Elias Takeda set out to learn whether shoppers buy more fruit when it is placed near the store entrance.","Takeda concluded that product placement may influence shoppers' choices.","Takeda found that fruit sales rose in the stores with entrance displays.","To investigate the question, Takeda moved the fruit display to the entrance in some stores but not others."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.62280280c8","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-165","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lucía Ferreira is a veterinary scientist.</li><li>Ferreira wanted to find out whether dogs recognize their owners' voices.</li><li>Ferreira played recordings of owners' voices and strangers' voices to dogs.</li><li>Ferreira found that dogs turned toward their owners' voices far more often.</li><li>Ferreira concluded that dogs may be able to tell their owners' voices apart from others.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Ferreira concluded that dogs may be able to tell their owners' voices apart from others.","Ferreira found that dogs turned toward their owners' voices far more often.","Veterinary scientist Lucía Ferreira set out to learn whether dogs recognize their owners' voices.","To investigate the question, Ferreira played recordings of owners' voices and strangers' voices to dogs."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.198b31be99","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-176","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Elias Lindqvist is a music researcher.</li><li>Lindqvist wanted to find out whether learning an instrument improves children's listening skills.</li><li>Lindqvist tested children's ability to pick out sounds in noise before and after two years of music lessons.</li><li>Lindqvist found that children who took lessons improved more than those who did not.</li><li>Lindqvist concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Lindqvist tested children's ability to pick out sounds in noise before and after two years of music lessons.","Lindqvist concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills.","Lindqvist found that children who took lessons improved more than those who did not.","Music researcher Elias Lindqvist set out to learn whether learning an instrument improves children's listening skills."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."}
